            <ArticleTitle>Bromodomains: pockets with therapeutic potential.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>477-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.molmed.2014.06.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1471-4914(14)00100-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Intense interest in the complex biology of the bromodomain (BRD) protein modules has fueled the development of novel small molecule inhibitors that target the acetyl-lysine (KAc) binding pocket of the BRD. BRD inhibition has revealed exciting opportunities for treating a variety of maladies such as cancer, inflammation, obesity, cardiovascular disease, and neurological disorders. With five BRD inhibitors already in clinical trials, the BRD field seems to be rising to success.</AbstractText>
